==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ang|en}} ''[[smoc]]''; akin to Old High German ''[[smocho]]'', [[Icelandic]] ''[[smokkr]]'', and from the root of Old English {{term|smgan||to creep|lang=ang}}, akin to [[German]] {{term|schmiegen||to cling to, press close}}. Middle High German ''[[smiegen]]'', Icelandic {{term|smjga||to creep through, to put on a garment which has a hole to put the head through|lang=is}}; compare with [[Lithuanian]] {{term|smukti||to glide}}. See also ''[[smug]]'', ''[[smuggle]]''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|ɒk}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A woman's [[undergarment]]; a [[shift]]; a [[chemise]].
#*'''14th century''', [[w:Chaucer|Chaucer]], [[s:The Canterbury Tales|The Canterbury Tales]], [[s:The Canterbury Tales/The Clerk's Prologue and Tale|The Clerk's Prologue and Tale]]
#*:Before the folk herselfe stripped she,
#*:And in her '''smock''', with foot and head all bare,
#*:Toward her father's house forth is she fare.
# A [[blouse]]; a [[smock frock]].
#: {{rfquotek|Carlyle}}
# A loose [[garment]] worn as [[protection]] by a [[painter]], etc.

